### Task 6: Reel renderer

**Files:** `packages/reel/src/**`, `packages/reel/test/**`

**Interfaces:**
- Consumes: `Storyboard`, `Scene`, `LineTiming`, `RenderArtifact` from `@dailies/core`.
- Produces:
```ts
export interface Timeline { fps: number; totalFrames: number; audioStartFrame: number; scenes: { sceneId: string; startFrame: number; durationInFrames: number }[] }
export function buildTimeline(sb: Storyboard, timings: LineTiming[], fps?: number): Timeline
export function toSrt(sb: Storyboard, timings: LineTiming[], audioOffsetSec: number): string
export async function renderReel(i: { storyboard: Storyboard; timings: LineTiming[]; audioPath: string | null; outDir: string }): Promise<RenderArtifact>
export async function renderStills(i: { storyboard: Storyboard; timings: LineTiming[]; outDir: string }): Promise<{ sceneId: string; path: string }[]>
```

Behaviour:

- **Timeline.** 30 fps. The title scene lasts 2.5 s and plays before the narration; `audioStartFrame` is its length. Each spoken scene starts at the start of its first line and ends at the start of the next spoken scene's first line; the last spoken scene ends at the end of its last line plus 0.4 s. The end scene lasts 3 s. Scene frames are contiguous and `totalFrames` is their sum. A line with no timing, or a storyboard that is not a title, spoken scenes and an end, throws rather than rendering out of sync. Cut points are kept at least one frame apart, so colliding timings still give every scene a positive length.
- **Subtitles.** One cue per line, shifted by `audioOffsetSec`, `HH:MM:SS,mmm` timestamps.
- **Rendering.** `bundle({ entryPoint: <src/remotion/index.ts>, publicDir: <outDir>/public })` with the narration copied to `public/narration.wav`; `selectComposition({ id: 'Reel', inputProps })`; `renderMedia({ codec: 'h264', outputLocation: <outDir>/reel.mp4 })`. The poster is `renderStill` at the middle of the title scene. `renderReel` writes `reel.mp4`, `reel.srt` and `poster.png` into `outDir` and returns absolute paths. `renderStills` writes one PNG per scene at that scene's middle frame, named `NN-<type>.png` in scene order; the review stage uses them. Its signature carries no audio flag, so stills are always drawn as the captions cut: that is the tighter layout and shows every piece of text a frame can carry.
- **Composition.** `Reel.tsx` maps each timeline scene to a `<Sequence>` and switches on `scene.type`. Audio is one `<Audio src={staticFile('narration.wav')}>` inside a `<Sequence from={audioStartFrame}>`, omitted when there is no audio. In captions mode the current line's text is drawn as a caption bar.
- **Scenes.** One component per scene type in `src/remotion/scenes/`. All colours, type sizes, radii and font families come from `theme.ts`; no scene hard-codes a visual value, so the art direction can change in one file. Text is clamped to the limits the schema already enforces. Motion uses `interpolate` and `spring` only. Shared pieces sit beside the scenes: `text.ts` (pure clamping, fitting, diff and count-up helpers, unit-tested), `ui.tsx` (frame, motion helpers), `config.ts` (composition id and props, importable from Node).
  - `Title`: repo name, window label, session and commit counts.
  - `Prompt`: the quoted human prompt, typed on.
  - `Terminal`: commands and outputs revealed step by step, a pass or fail mark per step.
  - `Diff`: the patch with added and removed lines coloured, counters for both.
  - `Stats`: up to six labelled values counting up.
  - `Flag`: the agent's quote, the verdict stamp, the recorded reality.
  - `End`: repo, verified and flagged counts.
  - Two rules tie the theme to the review stage (Task 9), and `theme.test.ts` pins both. Every entrance has landed before the middle of its scene (`theme.motion.settleBy` below 0.5), because that is the frame `renderStills` hands the reviewer; a prompt still being typed there reads as cut-off text. The backdrop keeps a luma of at least 0.12, because the review runs `blackdetect` at its default pixel threshold of 0.10 and a darker background turns every sparse frame into a black segment, which blocks the run.
  - Text is fitted by estimated glyph width, not character count, so a Cyrillic or CJK quote shrinks instead of overflowing. In captions mode every scene keeps a bottom inset free for a three-line caption bar.

- [ ] **Step 1: Write failing tests** for the pure parts, with a `fixtureStoryboard()` helper holding one scene of each type:

```ts
it('places the title before the audio and keeps scenes contiguous', () => {
  const tl = buildTimeline(sb, timings);
  expect(tl.audioStartFrame).toBe(75);
  expect(tl.scenes[0]).toMatchObject({ startFrame: 0, durationInFrames: 75 });
  for (let i = 1; i < tl.scenes.length; i++) expect(tl.scenes[i]!.startFrame).toBe(tl.scenes[i - 1]!.startFrame + tl.scenes[i - 1]!.durationInFrames);
  expect(tl.totalFrames).toBe(tl.scenes.reduce((n, s) => n + s.durationInFrames, 0));
});
it('gives the end scene 90 frames');
it('writes SRT cues shifted by the audio offset', () => {
  expect(toSrt(sb, timings, 2.5).split('\n').slice(0, 3)).toEqual(['1', '00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:06,100', sb.scenes[1]!.lines[0]!.text]);
});
```
- [ ] **Step 2: Run.** Expected: FAIL.
- [ ] **Step 3: Implement** `timeline.ts`, `srt.ts`, `theme.ts`, then the Remotion entry, `Root.tsx`, `Reel.tsx` and the seven scenes, then `render.ts`.
- [ ] **Step 4: Run the unit tests.** Expected: PASS.
- [ ] **Step 5: Render smoke test** `packages/reel/test/render.test.ts` with a 120 s timeout per test: render the fixture storyboard without audio, then assert the MP4 exists and `ffprobe` reports 1920x1080, h264 and a duration within 0.2 s of `totalFrames / 30`, and that `blackdetect=d=0.5` finds nothing. Render a short storyboard with a generated tone and assert the audio stream starts at the end of the title. Also call `renderStills` on a storyboard with every field at its schema limit and assert one PNG per scene. `ensureBrowser()` runs in `beforeAll` with its own timeout: the first run on a machine downloads Chrome Headless Shell, which does not fit in a test's 120 s.
- [ ] **Step 6: Open two of the stills and check them by eye** for overflow and empty frames; fix and re-render.
- [ ] **Step 7: Commit.**

